<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- W3C SCXML B.2.8.1 gives a payload three readings: interpretable as an XML
     document becomes a DOM, interpretable as structured data becomes the
     corresponding value, "otherwise" it becomes a space-normalized string.
     The third is a fallback, and this fixture is about what a fallback costs.

     A host builds `{"done":true}` and sends it. Something truncates it, or a
     serializer emits a trailing comma, or the field is a NaN. The clause is
     satisfied: the content becomes a string. The document then evaluates
     `_event.data.done`, gets nothing, and takes the transition it would have
     taken had the host sent a payload with no `done` field at all. Nothing is
     raised - the fallback is CORRECT behaviour, not an error - and nothing is
     counted. The run continues, one belief poorer, and says nothing.

     These two deliveries are what the document cannot tell apart:

       answer  {"done":                the payload never parsed
       answer  {"ready":true}          it parsed; `done` is genuinely absent

     Both leave `answers` at +1 and the configuration in `waiting`. The
     datamodel counters are here for the same reason `discarded_event_is_
     observable` carries its own: without them a passing run cannot be told
     apart from a run in which nothing fired at all.

     So the three deliveries the drivers send walk the readings the clause
     names, and the fourth proves the new count is a count of FAILURES rather
     than of payloads:

       note    just a sentence         rung 3, working - W3C test 562 sends
                                       exactly this and requires it to arrive
                                       as a string. Must NOT be counted; a
                                       diagnostic that fires when nothing is
                                       wrong is one nobody reads.
       answer  {"done":                rung 3, reached by content that asked
                                       for rung 2. Counted.
       note    [1,2                    the same, announced with `[`, and under
                                       a DIFFERENT event name - so a channel
                                       that reports "the last event" rather
                                       than "the last event that lost a
                                       payload" is red here rather than
                                       accidentally right.
       answer  {"done":true}           rung 2. Reaches `accepted`, which is how
                                       the drivers know the structured read
                                       genuinely worked instead of merely not
                                       being counted - and the count must not
                                       move.

     Rung 1 (XML -> DOM) is deliberately absent: `xml_data_is_a_dom_tree` owns
     that axis, and the ladder returns from it before reaching the rule this
     fixture measures.

     Deliberately single-axis: no delayed `<send>`, no `<invoke>`, no
     `<parallel>`. This fixture owns only what a host learns about a payload
     the datamodel could not read.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="waiting"
       name="undecodable_payload_is_reported">
    <datamodel>
        <data id="answers" expr="0"/>
        <data id="notes" expr="0"/>
    </datamodel>
    <state id="waiting">
        <transition event="answer" cond="_event.data.done" target="accepted"/>
        <transition event="answer">
            <assign location="answers" expr="answers + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="note">
            <assign location="notes" expr="notes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>
    <final id="accepted"/>
</scxml>
